            <ArticleTitle>Pectoral nerve block (Pecs block) with sedation for breast conserving surgery without general anesthesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>166-169</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4174/astr.2017.93.3.166</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Most regional anesthesia in breast surgeries is performed as postoperative pain management under general anesthesia, and not as the primary anesthesia. Regional anesthesia has very few cardiovascular or pulmonary side-effects, as compared with general anesthesia. Pectoral nerve block is a relatively new technique, with fewer complications than other regional anesthesia. We performed Pecs I and Pec II block simultaneously as primary anesthesia under moderate sedation with dexmedetomidine for breast conserving surgery in a 49-year-old female patient with invasive ductal carcinoma. Block was uneventful and showed no complications. Thus, Pecs block with sedation could be an alternative to general anesthesia for breast surgeries.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
